design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:5:title: Streaming restart loses conversation context
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:15:Continuing an existing MD² conversation with a streaming action can lose earlier context after its provider process has stopped.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:17:MD² stores a provider session with a `conversationId` and `synchronizedThroughMessageId`. The cursor means that the provider session already contains the transcript through that message. MD² therefore sends only later transcript entries when continuing that session.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:24:The new provider session receives only the post-cursor transcript and current prompt. Earlier conversation context is missing. Same-process streaming turns are unaffected.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:26:One-shot continuation already uses the saved provider conversation ID and retries with the full transcript when the provider reports that the session is unavailable.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:33:2. When no process is active and the selected agent has a provider session, resume it using its saved provider conversation ID.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:43:- Provider conversation IDs are provider-specific and must not be shared between agents.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:56:- Restarted Codex streaming conversation resumes its saved thread and keeps earlier context.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:57:- Restarted Claude streaming conversation resumes its saved session and keeps earlier context.
design\feature_descriptions\F_79_streaming_restart_loses_conversation_context.md:67:- Restarting a streaming conversation never loses synchronized context.
design\feature_descriptions\F_78_uniform_agent_controls_and_auto_finish.md:51:- Live output, selectors, conversation display, and status presentation remain provider-independent.
design\feature_descriptions\F_78_uniform_agent_controls_and_auto_finish.md:84:- Persist terminal conversation state before continuing the action chain.
design\feature_descriptions\F_76_action_streaming_or_oneshot_agent_mode.md:16:- `handleClose()` is the only terminal path: persists conversation, releases `runningConversationIds`, calls `onComplete`.
design\feature_descriptions\F_76_action_streaming_or_oneshot_agent_mode.md:23:Multi-turn today is **faked**: each turn is a new process, continuity comes from `buildResumeAgentCommand` (`claude --resume <id>` / `codex exec resume --json <id>`) plus transcript replay through `contextInput`. So a conversation = sequence of one-shot processes. One process = one turn = one `assistant` message (`${runId}-assistant`).
design\feature_descriptions\F_76_action_streaming_or_oneshot_agent_mode.md:53:  - new `sendTurn(runId, text)` writing the next user turn; append the user message to the conversation.
design\feature_descriptions\F_76_action_streaming_or_oneshot_agent_mode.md:56:  - `runningConversationIds` guard, conversation persistence, and `onComplete` all hang off `handleClose`; each needs a streaming-aware trigger.
design\feature_descriptions\F_75_agent_confirm_gate_in_one_shot_cli.md:33:- Keep JSONL transport and current conversation/activity model.
